
#import "../../../_lib/kodama.typ": *

#show: kodama

#metadata((
  title: "Huber ring",
))

#subtree(
  title: "Definition of Huber ring",
  taxon: "section"
)[
A topological ring $A$ is Huber if there exists an open subring $A_0 subset A$ which is adic with respect to a finitely generated ideal $I subset A_0$ of definition. Such $A_0 subset A$ is called a ring of definition.We make following definitions:

- A subset $S subset A$ of a topological ring is bounded if for all open neighbourhoods $U$ of $0$ there exists an open neighborhood $V$ of $0$ such that $V S subset U$.
- An element $x in A$ is power-bounded if ${x^n}_(n>=0)$ is bounded.
- An element $x in A$ is topologically nilpotent if ${x^n}_(n>=0)$ converges to $0$.
- $A^circle subset A$ is the set of power-bounded elements and $A^(circle circle)$ is the set of topologically nilpotent elements.
- A Huber ring is Tate if it contains a pseudo-uniformizer, that is, a topologically nilpotent unit $g in A$.
-  A Huber ring $A$ is uniform if $A^circle$ is bounded.
Fix a Huber ring $A$, the following holds:
- A subring $A_0 subset A$ is a ring of definition if and only if it is open and bounded, see #local("/ref/Hub93Continuous.typst", text: [Continuous valuations, Proposition 1]).
- For any ring of definition $A_0$ with a finitely generated ideal $I$ of definition, $A_0$ is contained in $A^circle$ and $I$ is contained in $A^(circle circle)$.
- The ring $A^circle$ is the filtered union of the rings of definition $A_0 subset A^circle$.
- The ideal $A^(circle circle)$ is the filtered union of the ideals of definition $I subset A_0 subset A^circle$.
- $A^circle$ is an integrally closed open subring of $A$ and $A^(circle circle)$ is an open radical ideal of $A^(circle)$.
- If $A$ is separated, Tate, and uniform, then $A$ is reduced.

#proof()[
  - A ring of definition is clearly open and bounded. Conversely, let $A$ be a Huber ring with $J$, an ideal of definition with finite generating set $S$. Let $A_0 subset A$ another bounded open subring. $A_0$ is open, so there exists $n$ such that $J^n subset A_0$. $A_0$ is bounded, so there exists $m$ such that $(S^n A_0)^m subset J^(n m) A_0 subset J$, that is, $A_0$ is adic with finitely generated ideal of definition $S^n A_0$.
  - Trivial.
  - Let $A_0, A'_0$ be ring of definitions, then the subring generated by $A_0, A'_0$ is bounded and still a ring of definition. Let $A_0$ be a ring of definition and $x in A^circle$, then $A_(0)[x]$ is bounded and still a ring of definition.
  - Similar argument as $A^circle$.
  - It remains to prove $A^circle$ is integrally closed. If $x in A$ is integral over $A^circle$, then $x$ is integral over $A_0$, a ring of definition. Then $A_0[x]$ is finite over $A_0$ and bounded.
  - If $x in A$ is nilpotent and $g$ is a pseudo-uniformizer, then $g^(-n) x in A^(circle)$. Hence 
  $
    x in inter.big_(n >= 0) g^n A^(circle) = {0}
  $
]

A subring $A^+ subset A$ is a ring of integral elements if it is open and integrally closed in $A$, and $A^+ subset A^circle$. A Huber pair is a pair $(A, A^+)$ where $A$ is Huber and $A^+$ is a ring of integral elements.
]

#subtree(
  title: "Pre-adic space",
  taxon: "section"
)[
The adic spectrum $op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ of a Huber pair $(A, A^+)$ is the set of equivalence classes of continuous valuations $abs(-) : A -> Gamma union {0}$ for totally ordered Abelian group $Gamma$ on $A$ such that $abs(A^+) <= 1$. Then $op("Spa")(A, A^+) = op("Spa")(hat(A), hat(A)^+)$ and the following holds for a complete Huber pair $(A, A^+)$:
- $op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ is non-empty whenever $A != 0$, see #local("/ref/Hub93Continuous.typst", text: [Continuous valuations, Proposition 3.6.i]).
- $f in A^+$ if and only if $abs(f(x)) <= 1$ for all $x in op("Spa")(A, A^+)$, see #local("/ref/Hub93Continuous.typst", text: [Continuous valuations, Lemma 3.3.i]).
- $f in A^times$ if and only if $abs(f(x)) != 0$ for all $x in op("Spa")(A, A^+)$.

We define rational subsets for each $s in A$ and finite subset $T subset A$ such that $T$ generates an open ideal $T A subset A$:
$
  U(T/s) := {x mid(|) abs(t(x)) <= abs(u(x)) != 0, forall t in T} = inter.big_(t in T) {x mid(|) abs(t(x)) <= abs(u(x)) != 0} subset op("Spa")(A, A^+)
$
Fix a rational subset $U(T/s)$, we make following constructions:
- Let $A(T/s)$ be localiazation $A_s$ equipped with a topology where $A_0[T/s] subset A_s$ is an open adic subring with ideal of definition $I A_0[T/s]$.
- Let $A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)$ be the completion of $A(T/s)$.
- Let $A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)^+$ be the completion of integral closure of $A^+[T/s] subset A_s$.
Then the constructed $(A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r), A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)^+)$ is a universal complete Huber pair associated to $U(T/s)$ such that $op("Spa")(A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r), A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)^+) -> op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ factors through $U(T/s)$.
- $op("Spa")(A, A^+) = op("Spa")(hat(A), hat(A)^+)$ identifies rational subsets.
- The rational subset of a rational subset remains rational.
- The intersection of rational subsets remains rational.
- The preimage of rational subsets remains rational.
We equip the category of rational subsets of $op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ with the Grothendieck topology generated by cover of rational subsets, called the adic site, then the presheaves: 
$
  scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))(U(T/s)) & := A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r) \
  scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))^+(U(T/s)) & := A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)^+ \
  & = {f in A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r) mid(|) abs(f(x)) <= 1, forall x in U}
$

#subtree(
  taxon: "expectation"
)[
  $scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))$ is a sheaf and for every $T subset A$ generating the unit ideal $T A = A$, the Čech cohomology vanishes for $r > 0$:
  $
    H^(r)({U(T/t) -> X}, scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))) = 0
  $
  In particular, $scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))^+ subset scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))$ is a subsheaf. See also #local("/ref/Hub94Generalization.typst", text: [A generalization of formal schemes and rigid analytic varieties, Theorem 2.2]).
]

Let $serif("CHub")$ be the category of complete Huber pairs, the covering on $serif("CHub")^"op"$ is generated by rational cover $X = union.big_(i in I) U_i$. We wrote $op("Spa")^(よ)(A, A^+)$ for the sheafification of Yoneda embedding. A pre-adic space is a sheaf over $serif("CHub")$ which admits a hypercover consisting of $op("Spa")^(よ)(A, A^+)$ under rational subsets.
]

#subtree(
  title: "Generalization and examples",
  taxon: "section"
)[
In #local("/ref/SchCla23Analytic.typst", text: [Analytic stacks, lecture 7]), given solid ring $R in op("CAlg")(serif("Solid")_ZZ)$, $f in R(underline({*}))$ is called:
- power-bounded, if $ZZ[T] -> R$ realize $R in serif("Solid")_(ZZ[T])$
- topologically nilpotent, if $ZZ[T] -> R$ factors through $ZZ[|T|]$
Then the set of power-bounded elements $R^circle subset R(underline({*}))$ is an integrally closed subring and the set of topologically nilpotent elements $R^(circle circle) subset R(underline({*}))$ is a radical ideal $R^(circle circle) subset R^circle$.

Moreover, given $s in R(underline({*}))$ and $T subset R(underline({*}))$ a finite subset, there exists an initial solid ring (which is acturally the $pi_0$-part)
$
  R lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r) := (R[x_1, ..., x_r]^(x_1^square, ..., x_r^square)/(s x_1 - t_1, ..., s x_r - t_r))[s^(-1)]
$ 
with respect to the properties:
- $s$ is invertible in $R lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)$
- $t_1/s, ..., t_r/s$ is power-bounded in $R lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)$
Huber's definition $A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)$ equals the "quasi-separation" of $R lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)$.

Adic spaces encompass schemes, formal schemes, and rigid spaces, see #local("/ref/Hub94Generalization.typst", text: [A generalization of formal schemes and rigid analytic varieties, Theorem 2.2]).:
- Schemes, any discrete ring $A$ is Huber and $scr(O)_(op("Spa")(hat(A), hat(A)^+))$ is sheafy.
- Formal schemes, an adic ring is Huber if it has a finitely generated ideal $I$ of definition and $scr(O)_(op("Spa")(hat(A), hat(A)^+))$ is sheafy if $hat(A)$ is finitely generated over a noetherian ring of definition.
- Rigid spaces, let $A_0$ be any ring and $g in A_0$ a non-zero divisor, then $A := A_0[g^(-1)]$ is Huber and $scr(O)_(op("Spa")(hat(A), hat(A)^+))$ is sheafy if $hat(A)$ is Tate and strongly noetherian, i.e.
  $
    hat(A) lr(chevron.l T_1, ..., T_r chevron.r) := {sum_(lambda in NN^r) a_lambda T^lambda mid(|) a_lambda in hat(A), a_lambda -> 0}
  $
  are noetherian for all $r >= 0$. 
Sheafy is defined in #local("../Adic.typst"), in above cases, the above expectation is true.
]